The existence of non-trivial solutions implies that the determinant of the coefficient matrix is equal to zero. That is,
\begin{align*}
\left|
\begin{array}{ccc}
1 & k & -1 \\ 
k & -1 & -1 \\ 
1 & 1 & -k
\end{array}
\right| &=0.
\end{align*}$Simplifying by expansion and reduction, we have
\begin{align*}
\left|
\begin{array}{ccc}
1 & k & -1 \\ 
k & -1 & -1 \\ 
1 & 1 & -k
\end{array}
\right| &= -1 + 2k = 0.
\end{align*}$Therefore, $k = \boxed{\tfrac{1}{2}}.$</think> <answer> $\boxed{\tfrac{1}{2}}$ </answer>

